  "configuration_id": "config_011",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best (config_009, acc=0.639) uses pca_n2000 + resnet50 + avgpool + weighted. All four completed experiments used pca_n2000, so the RNA encoder is the most important untested dimension. Avgpool+weighted is clearly superior to other aggregation/fusion combos tested. Question: Does replacing the simple PCA encoder with scgpt (a pretrained, biologically-informed single-cell encoder) improve validation accuracy while keeping the best-known image encoder (resnet50), aggregation (avgpool), and fusion (weighted) fixed? Why this experiment: It is the single most informative next step because it directly tests whether a learned RNA representation can push past the ~0.64 plateau, and it isolates one variable (RNA encoder) against the current best configuration. If scgpt helps, the remaining 5 experiments can exploit that gain across image encoders and profile settings; if it doesn't, we confirm PCA is sufficient and can focus on other axes."
}

